WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
My book is about entrepreneurship, but

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:03.680
what people don't understand about

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:05.800
entrepreneurship and leadership is that

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:07.360
it's directly applicable to working at a

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.480
company.

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.960
And I spend a lot of time with

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.360
high-performing employees. Almost my

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.440
entire network, there's just not that

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.440
many entrepreneurs in the world. So, a

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.040
lot of my friends in town, a lot of my

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.920
network is very high-performing

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.600
employees. They do their job very well.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.000
I talk to them about what frustrates

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.280
them at work.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.960
Or why they leave jobs.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.680
And there's a there's themes. There's

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:32.119
themes, and this is what not to do

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:33.280
if you're trying to lead and manage a

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:34.840
team.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:38.240
Number one,

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:40.040
if you make decisions slowly,

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.160
you are asking your top performers to

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:44.080
leave.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.840
And number two is if you tolerate and

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:47.160
surround those A players with B players

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.360
and C players and people who are

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.120
low-performing,

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.360
your A players are going to be gone.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.960
So, an A player, if they come to you and

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.400
say, "Hey, look,

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.480
there's a there's a better way for me to

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.800
do my job.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.960
I have this idea, that idea, and the

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.440
other idea. We can all agree

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.160
that this is the better way to do the

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.880
job."

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.760
And you as a leader, if you talk a big

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.760
game and do nothing,

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.160
that is the recipe to have an extremely

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.360
frustrated team.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.720
And a team that will eventually walk

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.920
away.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.360
And if it's a big office politics game

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.680
where there's one really high performer

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.680
and there's three other low performers

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.760
on the team and they've come to you as a

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.560
manager and says, "Hey, look, this

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.280
person's not doing their job.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.080
Hey, look, they messed up again. Hey,

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.560
look, this is making me work Saturday

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.720
nights because I'm picking up the slack

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.280
for this other person, and you refuse to

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.480
let that person go because either it's

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.800
too big of a company or there's a

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:39.640
bureaucracy. I know in some in America

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.960
we have a a very

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.240
um

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.520
open economy for letting people go who

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.040
are not doing their jobs well. Other

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.200
places in the world, the UK included,

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.720
it's very hard to let somebody go.

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.120
So, at major corporations, there can be

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.320
people who are low performers and make

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.560
it 40 years.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.800
There's nothing worse for high

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.680
performers. So, as a leader,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.120
that's just something to be aware of.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.360
If you tolerate those C players, your

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.920
entire organization will become a C

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.880
player organization,

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:06.320
and you're going to watch your A players

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:08.960
walk away.

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.280
>> You said something there that um

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:12.920
A players come to you and say, "Look,

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:15.240
there's a better way to do my job."

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.800
I think my worry would be

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.959
if I say, "Oh, cool. Okay, give it a

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.920
go." and it completely goes wrong. Do

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:23.480
you have to have some kind of judgment

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.480
around that, or do you just generally

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:27.320
say, "Go on, give it a go."?

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.959
>> Well, they're generally asking for a way

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:31.720
that you can make their life easier.

00:02:29.959 --> 00:02:33.400
So, generally it's your job, but many

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:34.920
times I'll say, "No, look,

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.000
I disagree.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:39.160
We don't need to be focused on adding X,

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.600
Y, and Z complexity to our business. We

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.440
need to do this, that, and the other

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.200
better, and there's lower-hanging

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.480
fruit." I have a lot of big idea people

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.480
that work for me, and I'm constantly

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:47.200
reining them in and saying, "Hold on,

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.240
let's focus. Hold on, let's focus."

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.400
There's a difference between that and

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:52.320
then saying,

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.600
"Damn it, I'm putting this stuff in

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.800
Microsoft Excel for 4 hours a day. Get

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.680
me an executive assistant in the

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.800
Philippines for $4 an hour to do this. I

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.920
make

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.560
I'm I'm one of the highest-paid people

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.519
at the company, and I'm doing this work

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:05.400
over and over and over again, and it's

00:03:02.519 --> 00:03:07.360
making my life suck." And the boss says,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.840
"Oh, yeah, I hear you. We'll think about

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.000
getting you somebody else to help you

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.760
with this."

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:14.800
And then nothing happens.

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:16.760
But I also think like from the employee

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.120
side, like

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:20.360
from the employee side, there are things

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.640
that you can do as well, which is

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:24.400
before you take a job,

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.000
is just understand, is it a big

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.000
conglomerate that there is no way

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.040
they're going to change anything about

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.519
the way they do business. A lot of

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:31.239
people A lot of employees walk into

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:32.680
these situations,

00:03:31.239 --> 00:03:33.840
and they work for one of the largest

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:36.800
companies in America. They go to New

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.400
York City and work for JP Morgan,

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.000
and they get in on the on the on the

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.720
floor in you know, the the 57th floor on

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.560
51st Street in New York City, and they

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:48.200
say, "Hey, um there's a better way to do

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.720
this, guys."

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.280
And the boss says, "Honey, we've been

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:53.440
making over a billion dollars a quarter

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:56.280
for 50 years.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.760
I don't need your ideas around here.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.920
So, if you're an idea person who wants

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.280
to move up quickly through an

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:01.800
organization,

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.080
don't go to the big bureaucracy. Don't

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.800
go to the place where you can hide

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:06.360
without doing anything, and you can last

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:07.760
40 years, and the person with the most

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.560
tenure who shows up early to meetings

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.680
and does the best at office politics

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:14.680
gets the most way you know, increase in

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:17.079
in salary. Work for a company where the

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.840
owner is aggressive, it's a small team,

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:20.400
the company's growing, and you can you

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:22.480
can do really, really well there. But,

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:24.840
another big part of it is just a

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.280
look down the hall at somebody who's

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:29.040
winning the game you're about to play.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.640
A massive mistake people make is they

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.120
pick the wrong game, the wrong career

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.800
altogether.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.520
Like, a lot of people, a ton of people

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:39.200
want to be doctors and lawyers.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:41.840
This is a very confrontational

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:43.240
or uh you know, not widely accepted view

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:45.200
that I have

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:48.000
is that I don't know very many

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.320
45-year-old doctors and lawyers who

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.720
aren't

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:54.160
obese,

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:56.000
overworked, divorced, depressed, or

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:58.400
addicted to substances,

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.280
but very rich.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.560
So, look down the hall.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:03.040
Look where you're headed. Look at like

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:05.000
if I play this game, and I'm about to

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:06.720
win this game,

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.760
and I'm very good at it, what happens to

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.919
me? Very few people who pick a career

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:11.680
actually

00:05:09.919 --> 00:05:14.919
spend the time thinking about, you know,

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.919
what does it look like if I win?

